  "id": "10-container-tree",
  "title": "Container tree for one Archon run",
  "description": "Container tree that one Archon review run (run-7f3a19c2) leaves in trace.db, and what Archon records for the same run.\n\n- **Top: Archon side · Archon DB** (violet), a 2×2 square reading right and down: workflow_runs row → run_id → review-codex (codex · gpt-6.1-sol) and review-claude (claude · claude-fable-5-1) node rows → workflow_events rows. All four Archon DB rows share one pink tile. These nodes get no kernel container. The workflow_runs row links down (leaving on its left, outside the frame) to the root container's top by `key [archonRunId]`. Stickies: stateFilePath() and the color key on the left; the Archon-only note on the right; \"why one container per invocation\" just above the parallel bundle.\n- **Bottom: Kernel side · trace.db + state tree** (green). Root archon-run container (proposed kind) sits just under the header. Five archon-node child containers in one row below; each child's `parent_container_id` wire leaves its container to the side, rises past the subsection's title chip, and joins one of two trunks entering root from left (round 1, round 2) or right (extract-findings, pack-context-a/-b). Each invocation is a yellow subsection holding its container row, pi_agent_sessions row → agent_runs row (pi_session_id · container_id), and its state.json path. pack-context-a/-b sit in the pink \"Parallel bundle\" subsection.\n- Color key (record kinds): blue container row, teal other trace.db row, orange state file, pink Archon DB row. All invocation subsections share yellow because they are one kind.",
